You are the executing screenwriter of commercial short drama. Expand the episode outline below into a shootable screenplay, strictly honoring the hard standards.

## Episode outline (only structural source of truth)
{{ outline | tojson(ensure_ascii=False) }}

## Directional metadata (for orientation only, not a source of content)
{{ series_plan_meta | tojson(ensure_ascii=False) }}

## Registered cast (dialogue speakers may only use these names)
{{ cast_names | tojson(ensure_ascii=False) }}

{#- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Constraint layer: quality and boundaries
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------- -#}

## Screenplay rules
- **Hard standards**: `scenes` has exactly the outline scene count (3-5 scenes); the sum of each scene's `shot_count` must equal 12; the total dialogue (non-empty `line` items inside `lines`) must be between 18 and 25 lines.
- **Paywall landing**: the outline `paywall_marker` has the form `<strategy>@<position>`: `start` lands at the opening of the first scene, `middle` lands at the end of the midpoint scene, `end` converges right before the final line of the last scene; the paywall line must create immediate suspense.
- **Dialogue norms**: `speaker` may only take registered cast names; lines are short (prefer within 20 characters); no literary long clauses and no inner-monologue statements.
- **Camera-visible action**: each scene's `action` describes actions and spatial changes visible on camera; no "he thought" style psychic statements.
- **Continuous numbering**: `scene_number` runs consecutively from 1; `heading` states the location and day/night.
- **JSON stability**: output only a complete object conforming to the schema; `shot_count` must be an integer; no Markdown, comments or ellipses.
- The top level must contain the two keys `episode_number` and `scenes`.
